tures raises the possibility of developing smart nanomachines with versatile controllability for various applications. Here, we overviewed the recent progresses in engineering DNA machines for specific bioengineering and biomedical applications.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Animals\n  * Bioengineering / methods\n  * DNA / chemistry\n  * Humans\n  * Nanostructures / chemistry\n  * Nucleic Acid Conformation\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding the Microtubule Cytoskeleton Piece by Piece - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25957410/)\nciteturn108search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; doi: 10.1074/jbc.R115.638452.\n\n. 2015 Jul 10;290(28):17154-62.\n\ndoi: 10.1074/jbc.R115.638452. Epub 2015 May 8.\n\n# Building the Microtubule Cytoskeleton Piece by Piece\n\nRay Alfaro-Aco^{  1 }, Sabine Petry^{  2 }\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n  * ^{1} From the Department of Molecular Biology, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey 08544.\n  * ^{2} From the Department of Molecular Biology, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey 08544 spetry@princeton.edu.\n\n  * PMID: 25957410\n  * PMCID: PMC4498055\n  * DOI: 10.1074/jbc.R115.638452\n\n\n# Building the Microtubule Cytoskeleton Piece by Piece\n\nRay Alfaro-Aco et al. J Biol Chem. 2015.\n\n### Authors\n\nRay Alfaro-Aco^{  1 }, Sabine Petry^{  2 }\n\n### Affiliations\n\n  * ^{1} From the Department of Molecular Biology, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey 08544.\n  * ^{2} From the Department of Molecular Biology, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey 08544 spetry@princeton.edu.\n\n  * PMID: 25957410\n  * PMCID: PMC4498055\n  * DOI: 10.1074/jbc.R115.638452\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe microtubule (MT) cytoskeleton gives cells their shape, organizes the cellular interior, and segregates chromosomes. These functions rely on the precise arrangement of MTs, which is achieved by the coordinated action of MT-associated proteins (MAPs). We highlight the first and most important examples of how different MAP activities are combined in vitro to create an ensemble function that exceeds the simple addition of their individual activities, and how the Xenopus laevis egg extract system has been utilized as a powerful intermediate between cellular and purified systems to uncover the design principles of self-organized MT networks in the cell.\n\nKeywords: cell division; cytoskeleton; microtubule; microtubule-associated protein (MAP); mitotic spindle.\n\n\n## Figures\n\nFIGURE 1.\n\nOrganization of the microtubule cytoskeleton…\n\nFIGURE 1.\n\nOrganization of the microtubule cytoskeleton by MAPs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTISSUE ENGINEERING: Part B (https://lmms.xjtu.edu.cn/__local/B/75/E2/ED8499BB47F0F42EC40B1938AA1_8B422E36_E7357.pdf)\nciteturn108search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; DOI: 10.1089/ten.teb.2014.0494 ... 1Key Laboratory of Systems Bioengineering, Ministry of Education, Tianjin, P.R.\nTISSUE ENGINEERING: Part B\nVolume 21, Number 3, 2015\nª Mary Ann Liebert, Inc.\nDOI: 10.1089/ten.teb.2014.0494\nEngineering Artiﬁcial Machines from Designable DNA\nMaterials for Biomedical Applications\nHao Qi, PhD,1,2 Guoyou Huang, PhD,3,4 Yulong Han, MD,3,4 Xiaohui Zhang, PhD,3,4 Yuhui Li, MD,3,4\nBelinda Pingguan-Murphy, PhD,5 Tian Jian Lu, PhD,4 Feng Xu, PhD,3,4 and Lin Wang, PhD3,4\nDeoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) emerges as building bricks for the fabrication of nanostructure with complete\nartiﬁcial architecture and geometry. The amazing ability of DNA in building two- and three-dimensional\nstructures raises the possibility of developing smart nanomachines with versatile controllability for various\napplications. Here, we overviewed the recent progresses in engineering DNA machines for speciﬁc bioengi-\nneering and biomedical applications.\nCurrently, medicine and therapeutics tend to move into\nnanoscale, speciﬁcally targe